<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Ceolred of Mercia</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">d. 716</span> · <a href="/centuries/8th-century">8th century</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Ceolred was king of Mercia from 709 to 716, the son of Æthelred. He fought <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ine-of-wessex">Ine of Wessex</a> at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/woden">Woden</a>'s Barrow in 715. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/saint-boniface">Saint Boniface</a> condemned him for his immoral life and for violating the privileges of monasteries, and he is said to have died in a fit of madness at a feast. Æthelbald, whom he had driven into exile, succeeded him.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/ceolred-of-mercia.jpg" alt="Ceolred of Mercia" width="320" height="432" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Margaret Dovaston. In a book by Walter Hutchinson, published by Hutchinson &amp; Co. LTD. · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Ceolred_of_Mercia_Dovaston.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Early life and background</h2><p>At the end of the 7th century the kingdom of Mercia covered what is now the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/midlands">English Midlands</a>, bordered by Northumbria, East Anglia and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/wessex">Wessex</a>. Ceolred's father, Æthelred, had come to the throne in 675 on the death of his brother Wulfhere, and in 704 he abdicated and went to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rome">Rome</a>, leaving the kingdom to his nephew Coenred, Wulfhere's son. Ceolred's mother was not Osthryth, Æthelred's only recorded wife, and he may still have been young when he became king.</p><p>The main source for the period is <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bede">Bede</a>'s Ecclesiastical History of the English People, completed in about 731, but Bede appears to have had no informant in Mercia. Ceolred's own charters and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/anglo-saxon-chronicle">Anglo-Saxon Chronicle</a>, compiled in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/wessex">Wessex</a> at the end of the 9th century, add some detail.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Reign</h2><p>In 709 Coenred abdicated in favour of Ceolred, went to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rome">Rome</a> and was made a monk by Pope Constantine. Historians have generally accepted <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bede">Bede</a>'s report, but Barbara Yorke has suggested that Coenred may not have given up the throne willingly, since kings were sometimes forced into holy orders to make them ineligible to rule, as Osred II of Northumbria was.</p><p>The reduced prestige of Coenred and Ceolred may have stirred unrest among the Mercian nobility, and the survival of a hostile account of Ceolred may point to wider dissatisfaction with the ruling line; much of what is said against him may reflect his treatment of the Church. Ceolred confirmed a grant to Waldhere, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bishop">Bishop</a> of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/london">London</a>, a sign that London was firmly under Mercian overlordship, though he did not treat it as his direct possession as later Mercian kings did. Three possibly genuine charters of his survive.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Death and legacy</h2><p>Ceolred died in 716. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/saint-boniface">Saint Boniface</a> later described him dying in a crazed frenzy at a banquet, &quot;gibbering with demons and cursing the priests of God&quot;. His successor Æthelbald belonged to another branch of the Mercian royal line. Æthelbald had been forced into exile during Ceolred's rule. Through Coenred, Ceolred was related to Saint Werburgh of Mercia, Coenred's sister.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Ceolred of Mercia” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
